From: Elian Doran <doran.adoris.elian@gmail.com>
Subject: [PATCH] power: supply: axp20x_battery: PinePhone: Vbus hold voltage 4.3 V

The AXP803 lowers its input current when Vbus sags to its hold voltage. It
held 4.0 V here, and charging from a PC's USB port the port gave up first and
power-cycled itself (host: "device descriptor read/64, error -71", "attempt
power cycle"). Megi's kernel sets 4.5 V in axp20x_usb_power ("Change Vbus hold
voltage to 4.5V", against USB-PD chargers resetting).

At 4.5 V, from a PC's USB-C port offering 5 V 3 A over PD, Vbus sagged to it
with about 1 A drawn (some 0.5 V lost in the cable and connectors): the
battery charged at 430 mA, at 4.3 V at 690 mA, at 4.2 V at 840 mA. Weak ports
are now protected by the input current limit the ANX7688 driver sets from
what the port advertises, so take 4.3 V. Allwinner's BSP uses 4.4 V for a
PC's port and 4.0 V for a charger.

It goes next to the PinePhone's thermal setup of the previous patch.

---
--- a/drivers/power/supply/axp20x_battery.c
+++ b/drivers/power/supply/axp20x_battery.c
@@ -1186,6 +1186,16 @@
 		ret = regmap_update_bits(axp20x_batt->regmap, 0x84, 0x37, 0x32);
 		if (ret)
 			goto warn_bat;
+
+		/*
+		 * Vbus hold voltage 4.3 V (0x30 bits 5:3, 4.0 V + 0.1 V steps): the
+		 * input current drops before the supply sags below it. Higher holds
+		 * back charging on a lossy cable; the input current limit set from what
+		 * the port advertises keeps weak ports from being overloaded.
+		 */
+		ret = regmap_update_bits(axp20x_batt->regmap, 0x30, 0x7 << 3, 0x3 << 3);
+		if (ret)
+			goto warn_bat;
 	}

 	return 0;
